.nh
.TH "EC_PROBE" "1" "APRIL 2026" "Notebook FanControl @VERSION@"
.SH NAME
.PP
ec_probe \- probing tool for the embedded controller

.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Probing tool for the embedded controller

.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.B ec_probe
.RB { read " | " write " | " read_bit " | " write_bit " | " probe " | " dump " | " monitor " | " evaluate " | " watch " | " acpi_call " | " graph " | " lua " | " shell }
.RI [ OPTIONS ]

.SH OPTIONS
.PP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
.RS
Show help message and exit.
.RE

.PP
.B \-\-version
.RS
Show program's version number and exit.
.RE

.PP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-embedded\-controller "={" ec_sys , acpi_ec , dev_port }
.RS
Select the implementation used to access the embedded controller.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ec_sys :
Use the ec_sys kernel module.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR acpi_ec :
Use the acpi_ec kernel module.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR dev_port :
Access the embedded controller via /dev/port.
.RE

.SH COMMANDS
.PP
.B dump
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RS
Dump all EC registers.

See
.B EXAMPLES
for sample output.

.BR \-c ", " \-\-color
.RS
Force colored output.
.RE

.BR \-C ", " \-\-no-color
.RS
Disable colored output.
.RE
.RE

.PP
.B read
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I REGISTER
.RS
Reads
.I REGISTER
from the embedded controller and prints out the value.

.BR \-w ", " \-\-word
.RS
If given, the value of
.I REGISTER
and the value of
.I REGISTER + 1
is read and combined into a single value.
.RE

.BR \-f ", " \-\-format
.I FORMAT
.RS
Specify output format of value.

.IP \(bu 2
.BR b ", " B :
Print value in binary.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR d ", " D :
Print value in decimal.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR x ", " X :
Print value in hexadecimal.
.RE

.BR \-m ", " \-\-map
.I FILE
.RS
Specify a map file (generated by
.BR "nbfc acpi-dump map" )
that is used for resolving register names.
.RE
.RE

.PP
.B write
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I REGISTER
.I VALUE
.RS
Writes
.I VALUE
to
.I REGISTER
of the embedded controller.

.BR \-w ", " \-\-word
.RS
If given,
.I VALUE
is written into a combined register of
.I REGISTER
and
.IR "REGISTER + 1" .
.RE

.BR \-m ", " \-\-map
.I FILE
.RS
Specify a map file (generated by
.BR "nbfc acpi-dump map" )
that is used for resolving register names.
.RE
.RE

.PP
.B read_bit
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I REGISTER
.I BIT\-OFFSET
.RS
Reads the bit at
.I BIT\-OFFSET
from
.I REGISTER
and prints its value.

.I BIT\-OFFSET
is the zero-based bit offset (0–7).

.BR \-m ", " \-\-map
.I FILE
.RS
Specify a map file (generated by
.BR "nbfc acpi-dump map" )
that is used for resolving register names.
.RE
.RE

.PP
.B write_bit
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I REGISTER
.I BIT\-OFFSET
.I VALUE
.RS
Writes
.I VALUE
to the bit at
.I BIT\-OFFSET
of
.IR REGISTER .

.I BIT\-OFFSET
is the zero-based bit offset (0–7).

.BR \-d ", " \-\-dry
.RS
If given, shows what would be written without modifying the register.
.RE

.BR \-m ", " \-\-map
.I FILE
.RS
Specify a map file (generated by
.BR "nbfc acpi-dump map" )
that is used for resolving register names.
.RE
.RE

.PP
.B probe
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I REGISTER
.I VALUES
.RS
Writes
.I VALUES
periodically to
.I REGISTER
while optionally reading other registers.

The register's original value will be restored afterward.

.BR NOTE :
This must not be relied upon as a safety mechanism.
Writing to the wrong register may still cause hardware damage.

Examples for
.IR VALUES :
.RS
.BR 1             " -- single value"

.BR 0xFF          " -- single value (hex)"

.BR 1\-100        " -- range of values (step = +1)"

.BR 100\-1        " -- range of values (step = -1)"

.BR 0\-255:5      " -- range of values (step = +5)"

.BR 255\-0:\-5    " -- range of values (step = -5)"

.BR 1\-255:*2     " -- range of values (step = *2)"

.BR 0,50,100,255  " -- list of values"

.BR 0\-100,100\-0 " -- list of ranges"
.RE

Presets for
.IR VALUES :
.RS
.BR all             " -- 0 - 255"

.BR bits            " -- 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128"

.BR all_reversed    " -- 255 - 0"

.BR bits_reversed   " -- 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1"
.RE

.BR \-d ", " \-\-dry
.RS
Do not write values to
.IR REGISTER .
.RE

.BR \-m ", " \-\-map
.I FILE
.RS
Specify a map file (generated by
.BR "nbfc acpi-dump map" )
that is used for resolving register names.
.RE

.BR \-w ", " \-\-word
.RS
If given,
.I VALUES
are written into a combined register of
.I REGISTER
and
.IR "REGISTER + 1" .
.RE

.BR \-r ", " \-\-read
.I REGISTER
.RS
Read and report
.I REGISTER
during the probe.
.RE

.BR \-\-read\-word
.I REGISTER
.RS
Read and report a 16-bit
.I REGISTER
during the probe.
.RE

.BR \-i ", " \-\-interval
.I SECONDS
.RS
Specify interval for writing values.
.RE

.BR \-t ", " \-\-timespan
.I SECONDS
.RS
Specify how long the program will run.
.RE

The options
.BR \-\-read " and " \-\-read\-word
can be used up to four times.
.RE

.PP
.B monitor
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RS
Monitor all embedded controller registers for changes.

See
.B EXAMPLES
for sample output.

.BR \-i ", " \-\-interval
.I SECONDS
.RS
Sets the update interval in seconds.
.RE

.BR \-t ", " \-\-timespan
.I SECONDS
.RS
Sets how many seconds the program will run.
.RE

.BR \-r ", " \-\-report
.I FILE
.RS
Save the monitored results to a CSV file.
.RE

.BR \-c ", " \-\-clearly
.RS
With
.BR \-r | \-\-report :
Blanks out consecutive duplicate readings when writing to the CSV file.
.RE

.BR \-d ", " \-\-decimal
.RS
With
.BR \-r | \-\-report :
Output register readings in decimal format instead of hexadecimal format.
.RE

.BR \-C ", " \-\-cpu
.RS
Record CPU temperatures.

A tool such as
.BR stress " or " stress\-ng
should be used to generate CPU load.
.RE

.BR \-G ", " \-\-gpu
.RS
Record GPU temperatures.

A tool such as
.BR stress\-ng
should be used to generate GPU load.
.RE
.RE

.PP
.B evaluate
.RI [ OPTIONS "] " FILE
.RS
Evaluate a report previously made by
.BR "ec_probe monitor --report FILE" .

This command calculates the correlation between register readings and CPU/GPU
temperature.

Note that the correlation may be reversed, meaning that higher register values
can correspond to lower temperatures, and vice versa.

The
.B ec_probe monitor --report
command must be run with
.BR \-C | \-\-cpu " or " \-G | \-\-gpu " or both."

The
.B ec_probe monitor --report
command must
.B not
be run with
.BR \-c | \-\-clearly .

.BR \-d ", " \-\-decimal
.RS
Read register values as decimal numbers.
.RE
.RE

.PP
.B watch
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RS
Monitor all embedded controller registers for changes (alternative version).

See
.B EXAMPLES
for sample output.

.BR \-i ", " \-\-interval
.I SECONDS
.RS
Sets the update interval in seconds.
.RE

.BR \-t ", " \-\-timespan
.I SECONDS
.RS
Sets how many seconds the program will run.
.RE
.RE

.PP
.B acpi_call
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I METHOD
.RI [ ARGUMENT ...]
.RS
Call an ACPI Method.

The maximum number of arguments is 8.

Example:
.RS
ec_probe acpi_call '\\_SB.PCI0.LPCB.EC0.SFSD' 0xFF
.RE

.BR \-d ", " \-\-dry
.RS
Print the ACPI command without executing it.
.RE

.BR \-m ", " \-\-map
.I FILE
.RS
Specify a map file (generated by
.BR "nbfc acpi-dump map" )
that is used for resolving abbreviated method names.
.RE
.RE

.PP
.B graph
.RI [ OPTIONS "] " FILE
.RS
Visualize recordings made by
.BR "ec_probe monitor --report" .

The
.B ec_probe monitor --report
command must
.B not
be run with
.BR \-c | \-\-clearly .

.BR \-d ", " \-\-decimal
.RS
Read register values as decimal numbers.
.RE

.BR \-\-register\-color
.I COLOR
.RS
Draw the register line in
.I COLOR
(default:
.BR blue ).
.RE

.BR \-\-cpu\-color
.I COLOR
.RS
Draw the CPU temperature line in
.I COLOR
(default:
.BR red ).
.RE

.BR \-\-gpu\-color
.I COLOR
.RS
Draw the GPU temperature line in
.I COLOR
(default:
.BR magenta ).
.RE
.RE

.PP
.B lua
.I FILE
.RS
Execute a Lua file.

This command is useful for experimenting with Lua while developing a
configuration that uses Lua.
.RE

.PP
.B shell
.RS
Read commands from STDIN.
.RE

All input values are interpreted as decimal numbers by default.
Use the "0x" prefix for hexadecimal values and "0b" for binary values.

.SH EXAMPLES

.BR "ec_probe dump" " / " "ec_probe watch"

.RS
